# Running the Project

The simplest way to execute the complete simulation is through the test program.

First compile the project:

```bash
make
```

Then run:

```bash
./test
```

The test program automatically starts the required processes and coordinates the simulation.

---

# Automated Test Configuration

The default test scenario uses the following values:

| Parameter                         |      Value |
| --------------------------------- | ---------: |
| Number of tables                  |          3 |
| Chairs per table                  |          4 |
| Total chairs                      |         12 |
| Number of visitors                |         10 |
| Maximum visitor stay              | 15 seconds |
| Maximum receptionist service time | 10 seconds |

The visitors are created during the execution of the test rather than all being treated as one single operation.

The monitor is also executed periodically so that the state of the tables and the orders can be observed while the simulation is running.

---

# Manual Execution

The individual components can also be executed separately.

The first step is to initialize the shared memory:

```bash
./main
```

This creates the shared-memory segment and generates:

```text
bar_key.txt
```

The receptionist can then be started with:

```bash
./receptionist
```

or by explicitly specifying its parameters:

```bash
./receptionist -d 10 -s <shared_memory_key>
```

Visitors can be started using:

```bash
./visitor
```

or:

```bash
./visitor -d 15 -s <shared_memory_key>
```

The current state can be inspected using:

```bash
./monitor -s <shared_memory_key>
```

Finally, the simulation can be terminated and the final statistics displayed with:

```bash
./statistics -s <shared_memory_key>
```

